digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-57012-4
  rankdir="TB";
  "Feat1" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Given the layer is produced by selenization and the transition is reversible, therefore the process is fabricable and reversible."];
  "Find1" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Given we present atomic evidence for Cu2Se to Cu transition and propose PSMT between distinct materials, therefore we demonstrate PSMT between distinct materials with atomic evidence."];
  "Gap1" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Given all reported PSMTs are same material and no precedent exists for different materials, therefore PSMT between different materials is an unexplored gap."];
  "Gap1b" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Given PSMT is an unexplored gap for different materials and usually temporary, therefore achieving sustained PSMT between different materials is a significant challenge."];
  "Gap2" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Given evidence is indirect and atomic proof is absent, therefore the atomic-scale mechanism is unverified."];
  "Gap2b" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Given the atomic-scale mechanism is unverified and PSMTs alter structures, therefore direct atomic proof of structural change is needed."];
  "Imp1" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Given PSMT has potential in applications and reflects fundamental mechanisms, therefore PSMT is important for both applied and fundamental research."];
  "Imp2" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Given PSMT is important for research, and the findings shed fresh light with applications, therefore this work impacts both fundamental understanding and applications."];
  "Mech1" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: Given light triggers face changing by sinking Se and lifting Cu, and knowledge says atom exchange switches to metallic, therefore the mechanism is interlayer atom exchange."];
  "Mech2" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-reasoning: Given the transition into excited state is crucial, and if barrier is lower in excited state then transition is crucial, therefore the excited state enables the transition."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper proposes that illumination of a Cu(111) surface bearing a single-layer Cu2Se film can drive an electronically excited-state-assisted interlayer exchange, in which Se atoms sink into the subsurface and Cu atoms lift outward, reversibly transforming a semiconducting Cu2Se skin into a close-packed metallic Cu monolayer and thereby demonstrating an atomic-scale photoinduced semiconductor-to-metal transition between distinct materials.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) The retained and repaired terminal conclusions jointly motivate the paper-level research proposal."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Photoinduced surface reconstruction with composition change is an underexplored opportunity, and surface reactions are among known triggers of surface reconstruction."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Because regulating surface reconstruction is critical and photoinduced composition-changing reconstruction is underexplored, studying photoinduced surface reconstruction that changes surface composition is well motivated."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Cu2Se is presented as an intrinsic semiconductor, and its thin-flake form is presented as a two-dimensional chalcogenide material context."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Because Cu2Se is presented in a two-dimensional thin-flake material context and photon interactions with two-dimensional materials can yield optical and photoelectrical properties, Cu2Se thin flakes are relevant to photoelectrical-property studies."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) Cu2Se provides a relevant material context in which this work reports atomic-scale evidence for a photoinduced transition from a Cu2Se layer to a single-atomic Cu layer and frames it as PSMT between distinct materials."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) The reported atomic-scale evidence for a Cu2Se-to-Cu photoinduced transition, together with the proposed sinking-Se/lifting-Cu interlayer exchange mechanism, supports interpreting the transition as an atom-exchange-associated PSMT between distinct materials."];
  "R20" [label="(0,0,0) For the atom-exchange-associated PSMT, the electronically excited state is an enabling part of the proposed transition pathway."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) The studied system combines a Cu2Se single-atomic layer produced by selenization of Cu(111), a reversible thermal return to the Cu2Se surface, and an excited-state-assisted atom-exchange transition pathway."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) This work proposes an atomic-scale, reversible photoinduced material transition in a Cu2Se/Cu(111) surface system that is relevant to both fundamental PSMT understanding and potential applications."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) The prior PSMT literature leaves two linked needs: achieving sustained PSMT between different materials and obtaining direct atomic-scale proof of the associated structural change."];
  "S1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently, the photoinduced semiconductor-to-metal transition (PSMT) has demonstrated significant potential in various applications."];
  "S10" [label="(10,0,0) If surface lateral atomic arrangement changes, then structural changes in surface reconstruction occur."];
